alt-syscall: allow newfstatat in "complete" on arm64 too

complete_whitelist[] lists newfstatat under "Unique to x86_64", but arm64
has it as well: asm-generic's __NR3264_fstatat (79), which is __NR_newfstatat
on a 64-bit arch with __ARCH_WANT_NEW_STAT, as arm64 is. And arm64 has no
stat or lstat at all, so it is the only path-based stat a 64-bit arm64
process can make -- glibc's stat(), lstat() and fstatat() all go through it.

Under the enforcing "complete" table on arm64-openfyde_vmware every one of
those returned -ENOSYS. The Notialis container (an Arch Linux ARM aarch64
userspace, pid 1 a /bin/sh script) started, but its shell could not find a
single external command: bash's PATH search stats each candidate, so
dirname, sed and sleep were all "command not found" and the idle loop spun
on "sleep: command not found". complete_permissive logged nothing but
"sh: syscall 79 not whitelisted" (and the same from sleep), and with it the
container came up and started its session.

Nothing on x86_64 changes: the entry moves from the x86_64-only block to
the block of syscalls both architectures share. Compared against arm64's
syscall table, this is the only syscall the x86_64 list allows that exists
on arm64 and was still denied there, apart from kexec_file_load.

--- v6.12-rpi.orig/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h
+++ v6.12-rpi/security/chromiumos/complete_whitelists.h
@@ -340,6 +340,7 @@
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(fstat),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(migrate_pages),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(mmap),
+	SYSCALL_ENTRY(newfstatat),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(rt_sigreturn),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(sync_file_range),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(umount2),
@@ -353,7 +354,6 @@
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(kexec_file_load),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(lstat),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(modify_ldt),
-	SYSCALL_ENTRY(newfstatat),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(stat),
 	SYSCALL_ENTRY(_sysctl),
 #endif
